  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65019,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 350,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mixed tumours typically range in size from 1.5 to 5&nbsp;cm and are solid, soft, rubbery, and mostly well circumscribed.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:12:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65020,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 350,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mixed tumours are composed of spindled and epithelial elements in varying proportions and arrangements. A cellular spindle cell component, with fascicular, corded, nested, and/or reticular growths, typically predominates and is sharply demarcated from the epithelial elements, glandular (typically mucinous) and/or mature squamous epithelium {15154010; 7682380}. Eosinophilic hyaline material may be present within a myxomatous or collagenous stroma. The cells show minimal nuclear atypia, and mitotic activity varies (although it is usually low). There is often coexpression of a variety of epithelial and mesenchymal markers, including AE1/AE3, CK7, EMA, CD10, SMA, desmin, h-caldesmon, WT1, calretinin, BCL2, and CD34 {15154010; 25998318; 7682380}. The spindle cell elements typically strongly express epithelial markers.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:12:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
